WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:05.590
In this unit, we're going to talk about
the very basic thing that a computer does,

2
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.970
which is execute one
instruction after the other.

3
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.910
We're going to look more specifically
at how exactly is that done.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.720
Well, really, it's very simple.

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.790
You first fetch the instruction
that you need to execute, and

6
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:19.680
then you just execute it.

7
00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.620
And then you repeat.

8
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.040
And that's the only thing that a CPU does.

9
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.960
Fetch an instruction, execute it, fetch
another instruction, execute it, and so

10
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.390
on and so forth.

11
00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:34.760
So let us look specifically at each of
these two stages, what it exactly happens

12
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.080
there and how does that translate
into what happens in hardware.

13
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.140
So let's first talk about fetching.

14
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.890
So, how do you get the neck,
next instruction that you need to execute.

15
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.110
Well, where is the next
instruction located at?

16
00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.490
It's in the program memory,
it's in the memory.

17
00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:50.680
Where in the memory?

18
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.800
In the address that's specified
by our program counter, probably.

19
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.550
So, we need to basically put
the location of the next instruction,

20
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.220
of the instruction that
you want to execute next,

21
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.510
into the address of the program memory.

22
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:09.430
And then we need to read
the contents of that memory and

23
00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.180
then we will get the basic
instructions that we need to execute,

24
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.000
the code of the instructions
that we need to execute.

25
00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.320
The next instruction will
usually just be the one after

26
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.340
the one that we just did in the previous.

27
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.370
That is if we now finished
instruction number eight,

28
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.530
usually the next instruction
will be instruction number nine.

29
00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:30.729
Sometimes we already know that we jump and
we're going to need,

30
00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:33.780
want to execute the different instruction.

31
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.690
So how do we actually go about putting
the address of the next instruction

32
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.730
into the address input
of the program memory?

33
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.220
Well, we're going to have to put it
somewhere and that's going to be into some

34
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.710
counter, into some register and that
is usually called the program counter.

35
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.820
So basically the hard words of exec,
executes the fetch,

36
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.240
if you wish, part of the cycle is
the following, there is a program counter,

37
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.130
when we need to jump into a new location
or we need to just increase and

38
00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.700
go to the next instruction, we need
to manipulate the program counter so

39
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.740
it will have the address
of the next instruction.

40
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:13.570
The output of the program counter
feeds into the address specification

41
00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.130
of our program memory.

42
00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:18.450
And then,
out of the program memories output

43
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.190
comes the actual code of instructions
that we need to execute.

44
00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:27.410
So that is basically what happens in the
fetch part of the fetch and execute cycle.

45
00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:32.750
So now, once we have the code of the
instruction, now we need to execute it.

46
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.640
So the instruction code itself has all the
specifics of what we're going to do now.

47
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.460
Which a,
calculations that we're supposed to do.

48
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.810
Are we touching this register or
that register and so on.

49
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.530
And whether we want to,
need to jump afterwards and so on.

50
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:52.040
So usually all that information is coded
somehow inside the instructions that

51
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.270
we already have because that we
got it in the instruction part

52
00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.810
in the fetch part of the cycle.

53
00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.108
And usually the way it's
coded is in a simple way.

54
00:03:01.108 --> 00:03:04.980
So that is basically,
different parts of the instruction,

55
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.090
bits, actually control the different
parts that we need to do right now.

56
00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.930
Sometimes it's more complicated, sometimes
it's more simple but that's a basic idea.

57
00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.070
So the basic execution executing
the current instruction basically mean

58
00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.120
taking the bits from the instruction
code that specifies what to do, and

59
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.200
actually doing what needs to be done.

60
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.950
Looking at it in the hardware phase,

61
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.390
from the hardware point of view, the
instruction we already got from our fetch,

62
00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:37.540
that is now going to feed into the control
bus of our CPU, of our computer.

63
00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.490
And that control bus,
basically controls everything.

64
00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:43.760
It tell the ALU what instruction
to compute, to add numbers,

65
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:44.850
subtract them, or so on.

66
00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.490
It also tells where do the,
data pieces come from.

67
00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:54.160
Do they come from which which register or
from the data memory or so on.

68
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.700
So we basically have the instruction
memory that we just fetched, basically

69
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:03.980
tells us exactly each part of the system
what to do right now in the execute cycle.

70
00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.190
And that really controls whether
we read from the data memory,

71
00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:13.180
whether we read from the registers, what
do we compute, where to write and so on?

72
00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.660
Of course there are many details.

73
00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.460
How is that exactly done?

74
00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.380
How do we, how does the bytes from
the instructions specifically

75
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.420
tell the ALU what to do which
registers to choose and so on?

76
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.730
That is best a, viewed through
the lens of a particular computer.

77
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.630
Specifically we will actually
get into these details

78
00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.600
when we talk about our computer,
about the HACK computer.

79
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.060
And that is what we are going to do,
be doing the next few units

80
00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.250
where you're actually going to see
how we actually control each and

81
00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.530
every part of the system
from the instruction bits.

82
00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.200
So, we're not going to delve deeper
into the specifics of this part,

83
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.870
which is really the most, more, most of
the work that is done and by the computer.

84
00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:57.290
But rather, I want to take a step
backwards and look at a certain problem

85
00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:00.930
that I've sort of glossed over so
far, and that is the fact

86
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:05.150
that we really have a clash between
the fetch cycle and the execute cycle.

87
00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.939
You see, both the program and
the data reside in memory.

88
00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.950
In the fetch cycle, basically we
need to get from the program memory,

89
00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.100
the next instruction.

90
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.960
So we need to put into
the address of the memory,

91
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.230
the address of the next instruction and
get the instructions output.

92
00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:23.230
On the other hand, in the execute cycle,

93
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.070
we need to access data that
also resides in memory.

94
00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.820
So we need to put into
the address of the memory,

95
00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.570
the address of the data piece
that we want to operate on,

96
00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:35.480
which has nothing to do with the program
piece that gave us the instruction.

97
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.050
And because we have a single memory,
that is a clash,

98
00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:41.300
because what are we going to put into
the address, are we going to put there,

99
00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.140
the pro, the address of the instruction,
or is address of the data piece?

100
00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.740
We need to do both and that's a problem.

101
00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.780
So how do we solve that?

102
00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.710
Well, basically we are going to do one
after the other, that is the usual way.

103
00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:54.990
How do we do one after the other?

104
00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.640
There's going to be a multiplexer that
feeds into the address of the memory.

105
00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.770
In the first part of the cycle in the
fetch cycle, we're going to actually set

106
00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.231
the multiplexer to plug into
the address input of the memory,

107
00:06:07.231 --> 00:06:11.770
the program counter that is
the location of the next instruction.

108
00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.830
While in the execute cycle,
the multiplexer will actually set

109
00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:20.230
the memory to actually point into
the data address that we need to access.

110
00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:23.950
And of course, the output of
the memory will go into both places.

111
00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:28.343
Both into the, the bit part of this
system that needs instruction and

112
00:06:28.343 --> 00:06:33.266
into the part of the system that are used
inside when executing to actually put

113
00:06:33.266 --> 00:06:34.647
information there.

114
00:06:34.647 --> 00:06:39.650
A, you may wonder, okay, so
how do we do both of them together?

115
00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.330
Well, when you look at it
you really need to remember

116
00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.830
the instruction from the fetch cycle
to used for the execution cycle.

117
00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:48.940
So that is what we're going to do.

118
00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:52.560
When we actually a,
are in the fetch cycle,

119
00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.170
we put the address of the next
instruction, we get the next instruction,

120
00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:00.390
and then we need to remember it
inside an instruction register.

121
00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:04.370
And that instruction register
is exactly what is remains

122
00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.920
holding the value of the instruction that
we're now executing in the execute cycle.

123
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.174
And then of course, in the execute cycle
now we have the instruction already stored

124
00:07:14.174 --> 00:07:15.162
in this register.

125
00:07:15.162 --> 00:07:20.044
And we can work with all the information
that we need a, for the execute cycle

126
00:07:20.044 --> 00:07:25.800
using the data memory that is being
addressed in the second part of the cycle.

127
00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.320
So, this is the usual way it's done.

128
00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.850
Now there is a shortcut and

129
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:33.280
that is simply keep the two different
parts of the memory separate.

130
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.660
That is,
have one unit holding the data memory and

131
00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.140
another unit holding the program memory.

132
00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:42.220
Each one of these units
has its own address.

133
00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.600
And then we don't need to worry about,
a, be,

134
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:48.360
between switching between them,
a, as we've just shown.

135
00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.290
Eh, this shortcut makes everything simple.

136
00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:54.490
It is sometimes called the Harvard
architecture, but we just view it as

137
00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:59.010
a variant of the von Neumann Architecture
and keep using it due to its simplicity.

138
00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:03.050
So, now we've basically finished
talking about the general a,

139
00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.270
architecture of computers.

140
00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:09.120
How in general do you actually a,
construct a computer?

141
